WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Overlay-style designs are a useful way to understand many RAM-backed systems. The lower

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.060
layer holds the base system.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.960
It may be a read-only filesystem image, an immutable snapshot,

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.460
or a mounted root that should not be changed during normal use.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.520
The upper layer records writes. When the system presents a merged view,

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.180
applications see what looks like a normal writable filesystem.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:27.040
Reads can come from the lower layer unless a file has been changed in the upper layer. If

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.980
the upper layer is tmpfs,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.720
writes are stored in memory.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.340
That makes the session flexible but volatile.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.440
A user can change files, install a package temporarily, modify configuration,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.300
or create logs during the session, and those changes can vanish at reboot.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.560
This is useful when the goal is a resettable workstation, kiosk, classroom lab,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.740
training environment, or controlled analysis sandbox at a safe conceptual level.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.440
It can also protect the base system from routine writes. The merged view is convenient,

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.140
but it can hide important design questions.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:07.800
Which paths are in the overlay? Which paths are mounted separately? Do logs persist? Does

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.300
/home persist?

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:14.080
Are machine identity files, SSH host keys, package databases,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.700
or application state expected to survive?

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.680
If the upper layer fills memory,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.660
what fails first? If updates are installed into the temporary layer,

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.320
do they disappear at the next boot?

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.580
Persistence must be deliberate. A design might mount /home from persistent storage while

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.640
keeping the system root volatile.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.740
It might send logs to a remote server. It might provide an explicit save action.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.800
It might update the read-only base image through a separate maintenance workflow.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.700
Without these decisions,

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.520
the system may appear to work until a reboot removes something important.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.540
OverlayFS and similar designs are powerful because they separate base state from session

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.640
state. They are also easy to misunderstand.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:02:00.540
Always test the actual writable layer, verify mounts, inspect what changes after a reboot,

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.160
and document which data survives.
